prime minister albanese you have  announced a public holiday to mark the  queen's death why was that so important  to you  i think it's important that we  commemorate the remarkable life and  indeed uh the service that queen  elizabeth gave to australia as our head  of state  for 70 years the longest serving british  monarch ever  but also a great deal of affection  from australians towards the queen  and on thursday the 22nd of september uh  protocol requires the  national remembrance day to be the day  after myself and the governor general  the  the now king's representative in  australia return  from the service in london  uh so here that will occur in parliament  house at the great hall i've declared it  a national day of mourning and together  with the states and territories we  agreed yesterday to have a national uh  one-off public holiday  the australian's relationship  with the queen appears enduring  what do you think it was about her reign  that achieved that special connection  i think the the queen was with  australians during times of celebration  the opening of this house parliament  house in 1988 the opening of the sydney  opera house  during  our great historic events  but was also with australia at our times  of difficulties  this is a land of cyclones or floods of  natural disasters  and queen elizabeth always reached out  uh to give that comfort uh to  australians at our time of need and she  was such a a respected figure  regardless of where people stand on the  political spectrum that 70 years of  public service that that devotion to  duty  is something that holds her in in such  high regard  how do you think the dynamic will change  under the reign of king charles  well of course it's the end of the  second elizabethan era and the beginning  of a new one  king charles of course is very well  known to australia he did some of his  as a student here  was in victoria  he's someone who's been a regular  visitor to our shores i think on a range  of 